kernel.c: four bocl_error() calls pass the message as FNAME

kernel.c ~2192, ~2207, ~2222, ~2243 (parse of external-format options):

    bocl_error("Invalid encoding %s",
               string_cstring(symbol_name(encoding)));

bocl_error's signature is (fname, message_format, ...): these calls pass
the human message as FNAME and the ARGUMENT STRING as the format string.
Consequences: the report reads "In Invalid encoding %s: <name>" — and the
argument-derived string is interpreted as a printf FORMAT (a symbol name
containing %s/%n would read/write through garbage varargs).

Fix: bocl_error(__FUNCTION__, "Invalid encoding %s", ...) — same for the
other three.  Found while renaming error->bocl_error (tcc-lane ticket);
behavior-preserving rename kept as-is, so this pre-existing quirk is
tracked here instead.
